You are an expert responsive web design evaluator.

Task:
You will be given screenshots of the same generated webpage at three viewport sizes:
- Mobile (375px width)
- Tablet (768px width)
- Desktop (1280px width)

Each screenshot shows the full rendered height and width of the page, but the region that is
visible to a user without scrolling is marked by a semi-transparent red border. Treat everything
inside the red border as the visible viewport. Content that falls outside the red border is
below the fold or to the right of the viewport edge and requires scrolling to reach. When
evaluating clipping, overflow, or truncation, assess whether content is cut off at the red
border boundary — not at the edge of the screenshot image itself. Content that extends beyond
the red border horizontally indicates a layout failure; content that extends below the red
border vertically is normal scrollable content and should not be penalized.

---

Scoring mindset:

Do not begin from a score of 5 and subtract. Begin from a score of 1 and increase only when
the evidence clearly supports it. For each dimension, your first task is to actively search
for failures — overflow, clipping, distortion, misalignment, crowding, incorrect reflow. List
every failure you observe before assigning a score. Only after identifying all failures should
you consult the rubric to determine which score those failures warrant.

A failure observed in any single viewport is not mitigated by good performance in the other
viewports. Score the worst observed case, not the average across viewports.

Score 5 is a strict zero-failure gate. It should only be assigned when you have actively
confirmed the complete absence of all failure conditions described for that dimension across
all three viewports. If you can identify any exception, deviation, or borderline case — no
matter how minor — the score is 4 at most.

Score 3 is not a neutral or passing score. It means the dimension has a clear, visible failure
that any observer would notice — a meaningful problem that degrades the user experience at one
or more viewport sizes.

Scores of 1 and 2 should be common in a typical sample of generated webpages. Scores of 4 and
5 should only be assigned after actively confirming the absence of all failure conditions.

---

Evaluation procedure:

For each dimension:
1. Actively search for failures across all three screenshots.
2. List every failure observed in the failures_observed field before assigning a score.
3. Identify the worst failure across all viewports.
4. Consult the rubric and assign the score that matches the worst observed failure.
5. Write a rationale grounded in specific visual evidence from the screenshots.

---

Dimensions:
1) Fluid Layout & Container Adaptation: The page layout and its major sections (headers, content
areas, sidebars, footers) should resize to fill the available viewport width at each screen
size. Containers must not clip their internal content, produce horizontal scrollbars, or leave
conspicuous blank columns beside fixed-width blocks. Judge this by looking at the overall page
structure across all three screenshots and checking whether sections fill the red-bordered
viewport cleanly, whether any content is cut off at the right edge of the red border, and
whether any section remains rigidly fixed in a way that leaves dead space beside it or forces
horizontal scrolling. When text, a heading, or a list item ends abruptly mid-word or
mid-sentence at the right edge of the red border without punctuation or a natural line break,
treat this as container overflow or viewport clipping — not as screenshot cropping. Each
viewport in which this failure appears counts as a separate failure instance; failures across
multiple viewports do not average out.

2) Flexible Grid Reflow: Multi-column layouts on the desktop screenshot (card grids, feature
rows, article lists, sidebar-plus-content layouts) should reduce their column count on smaller
viewports so that content stacks into fewer columns or a single column rather than remaining
side-by-side and becoming too narrow to read. Judge this by identifying every area that
displays multiple items in a row on desktop, then checking in the tablet and mobile screenshots
whether those columns have reduced and stacked logically, whether content order is preserved,
and whether every cell remains wide enough to be comfortably readable. If no multi-column
layout is visible in the desktop screenshot — meaning all content already appears in a single
column at desktop width — first confirm that the desktop screenshot does not contain any
side-by-side content areas, card rows, or sidebar-plus-content arrangements within the red
border that could constitute a multi-column layout. Only if genuinely no such areas exist,
assign a score of 5 and note in the rationale that no multi-column layout was present
to evaluate.

3) Responsive Media: Images and videos must scale proportionally within their containers and
within the red-bordered viewport across all three viewports, without overflowing the container
boundary or the right edge of the red border, showing a visibly incorrect aspect ratio
(stretched or squashed), or being cropped so severely that the primary subject of the image is
obscured. Judge this by inspecting every visible image and video in all three screenshots —
including media below the red border, since media below the fold is still part of the page's
responsive behavior — and checking whether the media fits inside its container and within the
red border horizontally, whether the aspect ratio looks visually correct, and whether any
meaningful portion of the subject is cut off by the container edge. An image or video that
maintains a correct aspect ratio but still extends horizontally beyond the right edge of the
red border is a responsive media failure. Correct proportions do not offset overflow; both
conditions must be satisfied independently. If no images or videos are visible anywhere in any
screenshot, assign a score of 5 and note in the rationale that no media was present to
evaluate.

4) Intentional Content Visibility: Secondary and auxiliary content (sidebars, supplementary
navigation panels, decorative banners, non-essential widgets, and similar supporting elements)
should be hidden, collapsed, or visually subordinated on smaller viewports so that primary
content remains the dominant visible element. Primary content is whatever the page exists to
communicate: the main article body, hero message, primary call to action, or product detail.
Judge this by identifying what the primary content is on desktop, then checking whether
secondary elements on mobile and tablet are taking up space within the red-bordered viewport
that competes with or buries that primary content. Pay particular attention to what occupies
the viewport on first load — the area inside the red border — since that is what the user sees
before any scrolling.

5) Proper Alignment and Spacing: Elements should maintain consistent horizontal alignment and
balanced whitespace between them across all three viewports, without elements touching or
overlapping unintentionally, and without large unexpected gaps appearing between adjacent
sections. Judge this by scanning each screenshot — including content below the red border —
for elements that share a visual column or row and checking whether their edges align, whether
padding and margins appear visually even within similar components, and whether any two
adjacent sections are either crowded together or separated by a gap that is clearly
inconsistent with the surrounding rhythm of the layout.

---

Scoring (5-point Likert scale):
| Score | Label |
|---:|---|
| 1 | Strongly disagree |
| 2 | Disagree |
| 3 | Neutral |
| 4 | Agree |
| 5 | Strongly agree |

---

Rubric guidance by dimension (use these to pick 1-5):

- Fluid Layout & Container Adaptation
  - 1: Any of the following is sufficient for a score of 1: text, a heading, or any element is
    truncated mid-word or mid-sentence at the right edge of the red border in any screenshot; a
    horizontal scrollbar is visible within the red-bordered viewport; a primary element such as
    a heading, main content block, or primary navigation is clipped or incomplete at the red
    border boundary. If the same class of failure appears across two or more viewport screenshots,
    the score is 1 regardless of how well other sections appear. A correct layout in one viewport
    does not offset a failure in another.
  - 2: Overflow or clipping failures are visible in exactly one viewport screenshot and affect
    a secondary or supplementary element rather than a primary heading or main content block.
    Or two or more sections across the page show obvious rigid behavior — dead space flanking
    them or sections that do not grow to fill the available width — without producing visible
    truncation or a horizontal scrollbar.
  - 3: The majority of the page fills each viewport correctly, but one clearly visible section
    either overflows past the right edge of the red border, is clipped there, or leaves a
    noticeable blank column beside it at one or more screen sizes. This is a problem score —
    the failure is visible and degrades the user experience.
  - 4: Nearly all sections fill their viewport and resize cleanly across all three screenshots.
    At most one minor area shows slightly rigid sizing, but it does not produce overflow,
    clipping, or a distracting blank column. Actively confirm no truncation or scrollbar exists
    before assigning this score.
  - 5: Zero failures. Every major section fills its viewport width fluidly in all three
    screenshots. No content is clipped or truncated at the right edge of the red border, no
    horizontal scrollbar appears, and no section leaves a conspicuous blank column beside it at
    any screen size. Assign only after actively confirming the complete absence of all failure
    conditions.

- Flexible Grid Reflow
  - 1: One or more multi-column areas retain their desktop column count on tablet or mobile,
    making columns too narrow to read, causing items to overlap horizontally, or squeezing cells
    to the point of illegibility. A single instance of this failure in any viewport is sufficient
    for a score of 1.
  - 2: Some multi-column areas reflow correctly, but at least one prominent grid or card row
    does not reduce its column count on smaller viewports, resulting in cells that are clearly
    cramped or hard to read, without reaching the point of overlap or complete illegibility.
  - 3: Most multi-column areas reflow to fewer columns, but one noticeable case does not — for
    example, two equal-width columns that remain side by side on mobile when each has become too
    narrow for its content. This is a problem score — the failure is visible and affects
    readability.
  - 4: Multi-column layouts reduce their column count logically across all three viewports and
    all content stays comfortably readable. At most one minor inconsistency exists that does not
    materially affect readability. Actively confirm no cramped or illegible cells exist before
    assigning this score.
  - 5: Zero failures. Every multi-column area transitions cleanly to fewer columns and then to
    a single column as the viewport narrows. Content order is preserved, no cell is too narrow
    to read, and no horizontal overlapping occurs in any screenshot. Assign only after actively
    confirming the complete absence of all failure conditions.

- Responsive Media
  - 1: Any of the following is sufficient for a score of 1, independently: (a) one or more
    images or videos extend horizontally beyond the right edge of the red border in any
    screenshot, regardless of whether the aspect ratio appears correct — a proportionally scaled
    image that overflows the viewport boundary is still a score 1; (b) one or more images or
    videos display a visibly incorrect aspect ratio (stretched or squashed); (c) one or more
    images or videos are cropped so severely that the primary subject is obscured. A correct
    aspect ratio does not offset overflow, and the absence of cropping does not offset
    distortion. A single instance of any of these conditions in any screenshot is sufficient.
  - 2: No image or video overflows the right edge of the red border, and no image or video
    displays a severely incorrect aspect ratio or subject-obscuring crop. However, at least one
    prominent image or video has a noticeably wrong aspect ratio that falls short of severe
    distortion, is wider than its immediate container without crossing the red border boundary,
    or is cropped in a way that hides a meaningful but non-central portion of the subject on
    tablet or mobile.
  - 3: The majority of media scales correctly and fits within the red border, but one noticeable
    image or video shows an obviously incorrect aspect ratio, overflows its container without
    crossing the red border, or is cropped in a way that cuts off a meaningful part of the
    subject. This is a problem score — the failure is clearly visible.
  - 4: Nearly all images and videos scale proportionally, fit inside their containers, and
    remain within the red border across all three viewports. At most one minor cropping or
    sizing issue exists that does not distort the subject, obscure meaningful content, or
    produce overflow. Actively confirm no overflow past the red border exists before assigning
    this score.
  - 5: Zero failures. Every visible image and video fits cleanly inside its container and within
    the red border in all three screenshots. Aspect ratios are visually correct, no media
    overflows its container boundary or the red border, and no subject is cropped in a way that
    obscures meaningful content. Assign only after actively confirming the complete absence of
    all failure conditions.

- Intentional Content Visibility
  - 1: Clearly secondary elements (sidebars, auxiliary navigation panels, decorative sections)
    remain fully displayed within the red-bordered viewport on mobile and occupy screen space
    comparable to or greater than the primary content, making the primary content hard to locate
    or access. A single instance of this in any viewport is sufficient for a score of 1.
  - 2: Some secondary content is reduced on smaller viewports, but at least one clearly
    non-primary element remains prominently visible within the red-bordered viewport on mobile
    or tablet and noticeably competes with the primary content for screen space.
  - 3: Primary content is accessible on all viewports, but at least one identifiable secondary
    element remains fully visible within the red-bordered viewport on mobile or tablet when it
    could reasonably be collapsed or hidden without losing important functionality. This is a
    problem score — the secondary element is clearly present and takes up space that the primary
    content should dominate.
  - 4: Most secondary content is collapsed, hidden, or clearly visually subordinated on mobile
    and tablet. The primary content is the dominant element within the red-bordered viewport.
    At most one minor secondary element remains that does not materially compete with the
    primary content. Actively confirm no competing secondary element is prominent before
    assigning this score.
  - 5: Zero failures. On all three viewports the primary content is the clear dominant element
    within the red-bordered viewport. Secondary and auxiliary content is collapsed, hidden, or
    visually reduced on mobile and tablet, and no secondary element occupies space that
    meaningfully competes with the primary content. Assign only after actively confirming the
    complete absence of all failure conditions.

- Proper Alignment and Spacing
  - 1: Multiple sections across the full rendered page show elements that touch or overlap
    unintentionally, or have dramatically inconsistent margins, or large unexpected gaps appear
    between adjacent sections — making the layout look visually broken in more than one area
    across the screenshots. A single viewport with multiple such failures is sufficient for a
    score of 1.
  - 2: Noticeable spacing or alignment problems appear in two or more sections: elements crowd
    each other, margin sizes are visibly uneven between similar components, or an unexpected gap
    between adjacent sections disrupts the visual rhythm.
  - 3: Most sections appear well-spaced and consistently aligned, but one area shows a clear
    problem — such as elements touching at one viewport, an inconsistent left-edge alignment
    within a column, or padding that disappears asymmetrically at one screen size. This is a
    problem score — the failure is clearly visible and affects the visual coherence of the
    layout.
  - 4: Consistent alignment and balanced spacing appear throughout all three screenshots. At
    most one minor inconsistency exists — such as a slightly uneven margin between two items —
    that does not disrupt the overall visual rhythm of the layout. Actively confirm no crowding,
    overlap, or large unexpected gaps exist before assigning this score.
  - 5: Zero failures. Alignment and spacing are consistent and balanced across all three
    viewports and across the full rendered page. No elements touch or overlap unintentionally,
    no large unexpected gaps appear between adjacent sections, and padding and margins appear
    visually even within similar components. Assign only after actively confirming the complete
    absence of all failure conditions.

---

Output requirements:
- Respond with exactly one JSON object.
- All scores must be integers from 1 to 5.
- The failures_observed field must be completed before the score field is determined. List
  every visible failure you found during your active search, or write "none observed" if no
  failures were found. This field must reflect a genuine search, not a post-hoc justification
  of the score.
- Each rationale must be one paragraph grounded in specific screenshot evidence (what you see
  on mobile vs tablet vs desktop), referencing the red border where relevant to justify
  clipping or visibility judgments.
- Do not mention policies, hidden reasoning, or speculation about the underlying HTML/CSS.

